-- title:
--   Theorem 3.5 — if $B_{r,s}(\delta)$ is semisimple, supersymmetric polynomials in $L_1,\dots,L_{r+s}$ generate its center
-- statement:
--   Let $r,s\ge0$, $\delta\in\mathbb C$, and let $L_1,\dots,L_{r+s}$ be the Jucys–Murphy elements of the walled Brauer algebra $B_{r,s}(\delta)$. If $B_{r,s}(\delta)$ is semisimple, then the elements $p(L_1,\dots,L_{r+s})$, $p$ a supersymmetric polynomial in $x_1,\dots,x_r,y_1,\dots,y_s$, generate the center:
--
--   $$
--   \mathbb C\bigl[\,p(L_1,\dots,L_{r+s})\;:\;p\in S_{r,s}[x;y]\,\bigr]=Z\bigl(B_{r,s}(\delta)\bigr).
--   $$
--
--   This is the walled Brauer analogue of the theorem that the center of the group algebra of the symmetric group consists of the symmetric polynomials in the Jucys–Murphy elements. By the semisimplicity criterion (Proposition 1.1), the hypothesis fails only for finitely many $\delta$ when $(r,s)$ is fixed.
--
--   **Formalization Note** "Generate" is `Algebra.adjoin ℂ` of the image of the supersymmetric polynomials under `evalJM δ`, and both inclusions are asserted. Semisimplicity is `IsSemisimpleRing`; the center is `Subalgebra.center ℂ`. The platform's product is the opposite of the paper's (p. 4); the center, the subalgebra generated by a set and semisimplicity are the same for an algebra and its opposite, and the $L_k$ are fixed by the up–down flip, so the statement is the paper's.
-- source:
--   Jung–Kim, Supersymmetric polynomials and the center of the walled Brauer algebra, arXiv:1508.06469v2, p. 18, Theorem 3.5

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_WalledBrauer_Center_Setting

namespace WalledBrauer.Center
open WalledBrauer.Blocks MvPolynomial

theorem theorem_3_5 (r s : ℕ) (δ : ℂ) (hss : IsSemisimpleRing (B r s δ)) :
    Algebra.adjoin ℂ (evalJM (r := r) (s := s) δ '' {p | IsSupersymmetric p}) =
      Subalgebra.center ℂ (B r s δ) := by sorry
end WalledBrauer.Center
